## 1. Introduction
For the past decade, the polymer solar cell (PSC) has been considered to be a promising photovoltaic device due to its
opportunity to improve light absorption and photovoltaic properties of PSCs based on nonfullerene acceptors. Very recently, the PCE of nonfullerene-based PSCs has been rapidly increased to \( \approx 10\% \), comparable to conventional fullerene-based PSCs.[3]
In particular, all-polymer solar cells (denoted as "all-PSCs") that utilize \( n \)-type conjugated polymers as electron-acceptors have shown promising device performances with excellent mechanical properties. Although considerable progress has been made in improving the efficiency of all-PSCs by tailoring p-type or n-type conjugated polymers, the overall performance of all-PSCs remains worse than that of fullerene-based PSCs. Thus, to further improve the performance of all-PSCs and to determine their operating mechanism, the effects of the chemical structures of the polymers, processing conditions, and device configurations on photovoltaic properties of all-PSCs need to be investigated.[4]
In PSCs using conventional fullerene-based electron-acceptors, the molecular orientation of the polymer donor has been recognized as one of the important parameters affecting the device performance.[5] For example, Osaka et al. has demonstrated polymer donors with primarily face-on orientations to afford high short-circuit current density \((J_{\mathrm{SC}})\) values with thicker active layers and without a loss in fill factor (FF) due to high charge mobility in out-of-plane direction.[5a] Moreover, it has been proposed by Tumbleston et al. that a face-on orientation of the polymer donor would reduce recombination of photoexcitons during charge separation.[5b] On the basis of these studies, adjusting the crystal orientation of the polymer donor is also expected to play an important role in making all-PSCs highly efficient. However, the relationship between the molecular orientation of the polymer donor and the solar cell properties of all-PSCs is still not well understood—in large part because it is difficult to control the crystal orientation of the polymer donor in the polymer/polymer blend system without inducing changes in the morphology of the blend, which is another important factor related to the charge separation and transport in PSCs.[6] Therefore, to clarify the underlying effects of the orientation of the polymer crystallite, detailed studies are necessary: in particular systematically employing, as donor materials, various conjugated polymers showing different molecular orientations. By comparing photovoltaic mechanisms depending on the electron-acceptors (i.e., fullerene
derivatives and polymer acceptors), we may able to find donor polymers with crystalline properties that yield all-PSC efficiencies comparable to (or even better than) that of the fullerene-based PSC.
Random copolymerization is widely used to influence the properties of conjugated polymer crystallites,[7] and we recently found random copolymers based on 5-fluoro-2,1,3-benzothiazazole with different amounts of \(2,2^{\prime}\)-bithiophene (2T) and dithieno[3,2-b;2',3'-d]thiophene (DTT) to afford various crystal orientations without significant changes in the polymer properties. Herein, we synthesized a series of random copolymers and characterized their photovoltaic properties in all-PSCs that were fabricated using poly\(((N,N^{\prime}\)-bis(2-octyldodecyl)-naphthalene-1,4,5,8-bis-(dicarboximide)-2,6-diyl)-alt-5,5'-2,2'-bithiophene)) (Polyera ActivInk N2200; hereafter denoted as PNDI) as the polymer acceptor (Figure 1).[4f,8] A systematic investigation was performed to understand the effects of the crystallite orientation of the polymer donor on the photovoltaic mechanism, in particular on the charge generation at the interface between the donor and acceptor, by comparing solar cell properties of the PSCs using [6,6]-phenyl \(C_{71}\)-butyric acid methyl ester \((\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM})\) and the all-PSC using PNDI.
## 2. Results and Discussion
As shown in Scheme S1 (Supporting Information), two homopolymers (P2T and PDTT) and three random copolymers with different molar ratios of 2T to DTT \((2\mathrm{T};\mathrm{DTT}=0.75;0.25\) for PR1; 0.5:0.5 for PR2; 0.25:0.75 for PR3) were synthesized via Stille coupling reactions in a toluene/dimethylformamide (DMF) cosolvent with \(\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{PPh}_{3})_{4}\) as the catalyst. The number average molecular weights \((M_{\mathrm{n}})\) and polydispersity indexes (PDIs) of the synthesized polymers were measured with gel permeation chromatography at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) by using chlorobenzene as the eluent. As listed in Table 1, all polymers were measured to have similar molecular weights in the range of \(25{-}33\ \mathrm{kg\ mol^{-1}}\) with PDI values of 1.66–2.55.
The optical properties of the synthesized polymers were studied using UV–vis absorption spectroscopy. The absorption spectra of the polymers in a chloroform solution and a thin film are shown in Figure 2, and their absorption properties are summarized in Table 1. In the solution, all of the polymers showed two characteristic absorption peaks in the visible region. The
absorption peak at the shorter \({\approx}450\ \mathrm{nm}\) wavelength was attributed to the \(\pi{-}\pi^{\ast}\) transition of the conjugated backbone, while the broad absorption peak between 520 and \(800\ \mathrm{nm}\) was attributed to the intramolecular charge transfer (ICT) between electron-donating and electron-accepting units.[9] Increasing the ratio of DTT to 2T yielded gradual bathochromic shifts from the spectrum of P2T to that of PDTT. This relationship apparently resulted in part from the increased backbone rigidity of the polymer chain in the solution due to the fused DTT unit; moreover, DTT is known to be a better electron-donating unit than 2T. The absorption properties in the solid state, however, were not much different between the polymers: all of the polymers exhibited similarly broad absorption bands in the range of \(500{-}780\ \mathrm{nm}\) with an absorption shoulder at \({\approx}730\ \mathrm{nm}\) ; and their optical bandgaps \((E_{\mathrm{g}}\mathrm{s})\) , estimated from the absorption onsets, were found to be similar, with values of 1.54, 1.54, 1.52, 1.49, and \(1.47\,\mathrm{eV}\) for P2T, PR1, PR2, PR3, and PDTT, respectively.
The electrochemical properties of the polymers were explored using cyclic voltammetry (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energy level of each polymer was estimated from the onset oxidation potential and was found to slightly increase as the relative amount of DTT in the polymer was increased, with the order being P2T \((-5.26\ \mathrm{eV})<\mathrm{PR}1(-5.25\ \mathrm{eV})<\mathrm{PR}2(-5.24\ \mathrm{eV})<\mathrm{PR}3(-5.20\ \mathrm{eV})<\mathrm{PDTT}(-5.19\ \mathrm{eV})\). The lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy levels were also estimated from the onset reduction potential and found to be very similar for the polymers, with values all near \({\approx}3.68\ \mathrm{eV}\). These observations were probably due to the LUMO levels of the donor–acceptor-type alternating copolymer being mainly governed by the electron-accepting unit (fluorinated benzothiadiazole in this work).[10]
PSC devices were prepared with an inverted device configuration of ITO/polyethylenimine ethoxylated/polymer electron-donor; electron-acceptor \(\mathrm{MoO}_{3}/\mathrm{Ag}\), where synthesized polymers were used as electron-donors and \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) or PNDI was used as the electron-acceptor. The device performances of the \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\)-based PSCs were optimized when the active layers (polymer: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}=1:1.5\ \mathrm{w/w}\)) were processed with a cosolvent of \(o\)-dichlorobenzene (\(o\)-DCB) and 1-chloronaphthalene (CN) in a \(97:3\ \mathrm{v/v}\) ratio. In the case of the PNDI-based PSCs, the best solar cell performances were obtained using 1:1.8 or \(1:2\ \mathrm{w/w}\) polymer: PNDI blends and \(1.5\ \mathrm{vol\%}\) of the 1,8-diiodooctane (DIO) processing additive. The \(J{-}V\) characteristics of PSCs under AM 1.5G illumination are shown in Figure 3a,b and relevant photovoltaic properties are summarized in Table 2. For both the \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\)- and PNDI-based solar cells, P2T, PR1, and PR2
exhibited higher open-circuit voltage \( \left(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\right) \) values than did PR3 and PDTT due to their deeper HOMO energy levels. The \( V_{\mathrm{OC}} \) of a PSC has been demonstrated to be proportional to the difference between the HOMO energy level of the polymer donor and the LUMO energy level of the electron-acceptor.[10,11] The short-circuit current \( (J_{\mathrm{SC}}) \) of the \( \mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM} \)-based solar cell was
found to gradually increase from P2T to PDTT, which is correlated to the decrement of the polymer's energy bandgap. However, when PNDI was introduced as the acceptor in the solar cells, PR2 produced the highest \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) value, despite its bandgap being near the average of the polymers tested, and PR3 and PDTT yielded the next-highest \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) values. As a result, the solar
cells with PR2 exhibited the highest PCEs of the PNDI-based and \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\)-based PSCs, the PSC with the PR2: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) blend displayed a PCE of \(7.03\%\) with a \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(13.2\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\), a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(0.74\ \mathrm{V}\), and an FF of \(72\%\), and the all-PSC with the PR2:PNDI blend afforded a PCE of \(6.01\%\) with a \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(12.8\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\), a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(0.76\ \mathrm{V}\), and an FF of \(62\%\). Figure 3c,d shows the external quantum efficiencies (EQEs) of the \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\)-based and PNDI-based devices measured under monochromatic light. The \(J_{\mathrm{SC,EQE}}\) values calculated by integrating the EQE spectra were consistent with \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values obtained from the \(J{-}V\) curves. In the \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\)-based PSCs, all polymers exhibited relatively similar photoresponses, with high EQE values of over \(50\%\) in the range from 400 to \(700\ \mathrm{nm}\), although the EQE of the P2T-containing PSC was observed to be a little lower than those of the PSCs of the other polymers. By contrast, the EQE spectra of the PNDI-based all-PSCs differed fairly substantially for the different donor polymers. In the range of \(550{-}720\ \mathrm{nm}\), PR2 displayed the highest EQEs, close to \(60\%\), and PR3 showed the second-highest values. The EQE values of P2T and PR1 were relatively low, below \(45\%\). The EQE values in that range thus appeared to be positively associated with the \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values of the PSCs. The different EQE signals in PSCs have been frequently explained by light absorption and (or) morphological properties of photoactive layer.[12] We therefore carried out UV–vis absorption spectroscopy and transmission electron microscopy analyses of the blend films. As shown in Figure S2a,b (Supporting Information), the various donor polymers yielded quite similar absorption spectra in the range of \(550{-}720\ \mathrm{nm}\). Moreover, as shown in Figure S2c (Supporting Information), all of the blend films similarly formed well-developed interconnected networks with a nanoscale fibril structure. These results suggest that the main reason for different photocurrent properties among our PNDI-based all-PSCs in this study are not due to the light absorption and film morphology.
To compare the molecular packing structures of the \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) and PNDI blend films, grazing incidence X-ray diffraction (GIXRD) experiments were performed. Figure 4a–d shows GIXRD data along with the out-of-plane \((q_{\mathrm{z}})\) and in-plane \((q_{\mathrm{xy}})\) profiles of pristine polymer films. In all of the polymer films, the (100) and (010) diffraction peaks were observed at \(q_{\mathrm{z}}=0.28\ \mathrm{\AA^{-1}}\) and \(q_{\mathrm{xy}}=1.72–1.73\ \mathrm{\AA^{-1}}\), respectively. These observations indicated that the interchain distance \((22.4\ \mathrm{\AA})\) and the \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking distance \((3.63{-}3.65\ \mathrm{\AA})\) were similar in all of the synthesized polymers. Meanwhile, polymer orientations varied depending on the polymer. All polymers exhibited (h00) diffraction peaks in the \(q_{\mathrm{z}}\) scan, associated with the edge-on-oriented crystallites of the polymer. However, compared with the PR2, PR3, and PDTT polymers, P2T and PR1 yielded sharper and more intense (200), (300), and (400) reflections, indicating the P2T and PR1 polymers to have a stronger preference than the other polymers to adopt edge-on orientations relative to the substrate. In particular, most crystallites of the P2T polymer appeared to have an edge-on orientation, as evidenced by the lack of (010) diffraction in the \(q_{\mathrm{z}}\) direction and very weak (100) diffraction in the \(q_{\mathrm{xy}}\) direction. PR2, PR3, and PDTT yielded more discernible (010) diffraction peaks in the \(q_{\mathrm{z}}\) scan than did P2T and PR1, implying that PR2, PR3, and PDTT produced larger amount of face-on oriented crystallites than did P2T and PR1. More importantly, of all the polymers tested, PR2 yielded the strongest
(010) peak in the \(q_{\mathrm{z}}\) scan, indicating that this polymer produced the largest population of face-on crystallites. Thus, from the GIXRD results of the pristine polymer films, we conclude that the population of face-on-oriented crystallites followed the order \(\mathrm{PR}2>\mathrm{PR}3\geq\mathrm{PDTT}>\mathrm{PR}1>\mathrm{P2T}\). This crystal orientation trend observed in the pristine polymer films was maintained when the polymers were blended with \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) or PNDI (Figure 4e–l): the crystallites of P2T showed a well-developed edge-on structure with obvious \((h00)\) reflection peaks and no (010) signal in the \(q_{\mathrm{z}}\) scan, whereas PR2 crystallites were found to display both edge-on and face-on orientations, i.e., a so-called mixed orientation, with a distinctive (010) diffraction peak in the \(q_{\mathrm{z}}\) direction. We also calculated the relative intensities of in-plane and out-of-plane (100) peaks \(((100)_{\mathrm{in-plane}}/(\mathrm{100})_{\mathrm{out-of-plane}})\) to quantify the face-on to edge-on crystallite ratio.[13a,6f] As shown in Table S1 (Supporting Information), in pristine, polymer: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) and polymer:PNDI blend films, P2T and PR2 showed the highest and the lowest values, respectively, and the order of \((100)_{\mathrm{in-plane}}/(\mathrm{100})_{\mathrm{out-of-plane}}\) values was well consistent to the result estimated above. In all of the polymer: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) and polymer:PNDI blend films tested, the (100) and (010) diffraction peaks appeared at \(q_{\mathrm{z}}=0.27{-}0.28\ \mathrm{\AA^{-1}}\) and \(q_{\mathrm{xy}}=1.72{-}1.74\ \mathrm{\AA^{-1}}\), respectively. These results indicated that the packing properties, such as the interchain distances and the \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking distances, of the polymer donors found in the crystallites of the pristine film were well retained after these polymers were blended with the \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) and PNDI acceptors. Meanwhile, PNDI showed strong (100) diffraction at \(q_{\mathrm{xy}}=0.25\ \mathrm{\AA^{-1}}\), while the polymer: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) blend showed diffuse halos at \(1.4\ \mathrm{\AA^{-1}}\) corresponding to the scattering of amorphous \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\). This difference may have been due to the PNDI polymer acceptor in the polymer:PNDI blend films having anisotropic characteristics with a strong tendency to adopt face-on orientations relative to the substrate, but the \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) acceptor showing more isotropic molecular arrangements in the polymer: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) film. Such distinctive characteristics of the PNDI acceptor have also been reported by several other research groups.[13]
We plotted the EQE values at the donor's maximum absorption wavelength and the \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values of both the \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\)-based PSCs (Figure 5a) and the PNDI-based all-PSCs (Figure 5b) for the various polymer donors in the order of their face-on-oriented populations in the polymer:acceptor film. For the PNDI-based all-PSCs, both the EQE and \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values were found to increase as the population of face-on-oriented crystallites within the polymer donor increased. However, no such trends were observed for the \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\)-based PSCs, although slightly lower \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values of P2T and PR1 were observed, due to their relatively large \(E_{\mathrm{g}}\mathrm{s}\). The molecular orientation of the polymer donor apparently had a greater influence on the photocurrent density in the all-PSCs than in the conventional fullerene-based devices.
To attain a deeper understanding of the effects of the polymer orientation on the photocurrent, we investigated the charge-transport abilities of the polymer donors in the blend films. Hole mobilities of the donor:acceptor blends were measured from the dark \(J{-}V\) curves of hole-only devices (ITO/poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene):poly(styrenesulfonate)/photoactive layer/Au) using the space-charge limited current (SCLC) model (Figure S6, Supporting Information, and Table 2).
Compared with polymer donor:PNDI blend films, higher hole mobilities were observed for the polymer donor: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) blend films, suggesting that the polymer donors formed better charge-transport pathways when blended with \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) than when blended with the PNDI acceptor. Among the polymer donors, PR2 showed the highest hole mobilities of \(4.71\times10^{-4}\) and \(7.69\times10^{-5}\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\;\mathrm{V}^{-1}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) for the polymer donor: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) and polymer donor:PNDI, respectively. This may be attributed to that PR2 has the strongest tendency to take face-on orientation in the blend films, which allows favorable pathways for efficient charge transport. However, the other polymers yielded similar hole-transport properties in their blend films despite having different relative amounts of the face-on orientation. Moreover,
we could not find any correlation between the EQE result and charge-transport ability in the PNDI-based PSC. These results suggest that the charge-carrier mobility does not affect EQE a lot in the PNDI-based all-PSCs. However it should be noted that overall hole mobilities in these blend systems are too low to conclude exactly the contribution of the charge mobility on EQE in the all-PSC. The detailed investigation on effects of charge transport and the charge generation on EQE are necessary.
Charge transfer and exciton dissociation at the electron-donor/electron-acceptor interface in all-PSCs have been recently reported to be considerably affected by their molecular orientations.[6a,b] The charge transfer at the electron-donor/electron-acceptor interface was investigated by examining
the quenching of photoluminescence (PL) in the blend films. Figure S7 (Supporting Information) shows PL spectra obtained from excitation at the wavelength of maximum absorption of the polymer donors, and their PL quenching efficiencies are summarized in Figure 6a. When blended with \(\mathsf{P C}_{71}\mathsf{B M}\), all of the polymer donors showed high PL quenching efficiencies, with values over \(90\%\), indicating that efficient charge transfer occurred at the polymer donor/ \(\mathsf{P C}_{71}\mathsf{B M}\) interface for all polymer donors. However, quite different PL quenching efficiencies were observed for the different polymer donors when blended with PNDI: here, only PR2 \((89\%)\), and to a lesser degree PR3 \((84\%)\), showed PL quenching yields somewhat similar to those of the \(\mathsf{P C}_{71}\mathsf{B M}\)-based blend films; in contrast, PDTT and especially P2T and PR1, when blended with the PNDI acceptor, showed considerably lower PL quenching efficiencies, at \(79\%\), \(56\%\), and \(64\%\), respectively. Therefore, we concluded that increasing the relative amount of face-on-oriented crystallites in the polymer donor resulted in more efficient charge generation in the PNDI-based all-PSCs by improving charge transfer and dissociation between the polymer donor and the PNDI acceptor. Other factors can in general influence charge transfer and dissociation at an acceptor–donor interface, such as an energy offset between the LUMO energy levels of the polymer donor and acceptor
and the morphology of the photoactive layers. However, these factors can be excluded in the current samples because of the relatively high similarity of the LUMO levels of these polymer donors (Table 1) and the similarity of the morphological properties of the blend films of the polymer donors as shown in Figure S2c (Supporting Information).[12,14] Charge-carrier generation in the solar cells was apparently more dependent on the intermolecular orientation of the polymer donors and acceptors for the all-PSCs than for the PCBM-based PSCs. This difference resulted from the different molecular structures of PNDI and PCBM: 2D \(\pi\)-conjugated polymer acceptors such as PNDI are unfavorable for receiving an electron efficiently from the polymer donor in all directions due to its anisotropic structure; whereas spherical fullerene-based acceptors such as PCBM can induce efficient exciton dissociation at the electron-donor/electron-acceptor interface regardless of the intermolecular orientation between the donor and acceptor. As a result, in all-PSCs using the PNDI acceptor, the same molecular orientation between the polymer donor and acceptor, i.e., face-on/face-on, is necessary for producing effective orbital overlaps at the
donor/acceptor interface[64,15] and thus for producing efficient charge transfer (Figure S9, Supporting Information).
To study the exciton dissociation behavior in PSCs in detail, we measured the photocurrent density \((J_{\mathrm{ph}})\) as a function of effective voltage \((V_{\mathrm{eff}})\) (Figure S8, Supporting Information). Note that \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}=J_{\mathrm{L}}-J_{\mathrm{D}}\), where \(J_{\mathrm{L}}\) and \(J_{\mathrm{D}}\) are the current densities under illumination at \(100\,\mathrm{\mW\cm}^{-2}\) and in dark conditions, respectively, and \(V_{\mathrm{eff}}=V_{0}-V_{\mathrm{a}}\), where \(V_{0}\) is the voltage when \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}=0\) and \(V_{\mathrm{a}}\) is the applied bias. The extracted exciton dissociation probability \((P(E,T))\) under a short-circuit condition was calculated by using the equation \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}=J_{\mathrm{sat}}\cdot P(E,T)\), where \(J_{\mathrm{sat}}\) is the saturation photocurrent density.[16,17] As shown in Figure 6b and Table S2 (Supporting Information), all of the PSCs based on the polymer:PCBM blend films achieved high \(P(E,T)\) values, of at least \(94\%\), with PR2 yielding the highest value of \(98\%\). In contrast, PSCs using the PNDI acceptor exhibited more of a variety of \(P(E,T)\) values. P2T:PNDI and PR1:PNDI blends exhibited low \(P(E,T)\) values of \(84\%\) and \(89\%\), respectively, indicating their inferior charge-generation capabilities. The \(P(E,T)\) value of the PR2:PNDI-based PSC, at \(97\%\), was the highest observed value among all of the PNDI-based PSCs and was nearly the same as that of the PR2: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\)-based PSC. These results further confirmed that the face-on-oriented crystallites of the polymer donors played a critical role in producing efficient exciton dissociation/charge generation in PNDI-based all-PSCs and thus in producing high photocurrent densities. Controlling the molecular orientation of the polymer donor therefore appears to be important for achieving high PCEs of all-PSCs. This is almost the first example which systematically compares PCBM and polymer acceptor-based solar cells using a series of polymer donors showing various polymer orientations.
## 3. Conclusion
Five random copolymers showing various crystal orientations in the film were synthesized and all-PSCs were fabricated using the synthesized polymer as the electron-donor and PNDI as the polymeric electron-acceptor. We studied the effects of the molecular orientation of the polymer donor on the photovoltaic properties of the all-PSCs. Charge-carrier generation in the solar cells was found to be more significantly dependent on the orientation of the polymer donor for the PNDI-based PSCs than for the \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\)-based devices. In contrast to the case for the isotropic \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) structure, efficient charge transfer from the polymeric donor to the anisotropic 2D-conjugated PNDI polymeric acceptor was found to be highly dependent on correlated orientation between the polymer acceptor and the polymer donor crystallite, due to this orientation inducing effective intermolecular orbital overlapping between the donor and acceptor. P2T, whose crystallite was found to be predominantly oriented edge-on in the film, therefore showed a large reduction in the EQE and PCE values when the acceptor PNDI, which prefers a face-on orientation, was replaced for the acceptor \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) in the PSCs (PCE of \(\mathrm{P2T:PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}=\) \(5.98\%\), PCE of \(\mathrm{P2T:PNDI}=3.40\%\)). In contrast, PR2, which was found to have the largest population of face-on-oriented crystallites among the polymer donors, exhibited high EQEs (of \(\approx60\%\)) at its maximum absorption wavelength and exhibited the highest solar cell efficiencies in both \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\)-based and PNDI-based devices (PCE of \(\mathrm{PR2:PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}=7.03\%\), PCE of \(\mathrm{PR2:PNDI}=6.01\%\)). Controlling the crystal orientation of the donor polymer to face the accepting polymer in all-PSCs is therefore important for designing new electron-donating or electron-accepting polymers for the purpose of achieving efficient charge generation and thus high solar cell performance.
